WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.680
As a Sun-like giant loses its envelope, a

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:05.720
small, hot core is exposed. The expanding gas

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.140
and the central remnant are different objects. The

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.400
remnant becomes a white dwarf, roughly Earth-sized, which

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.820
gradually cools and fades. A planetary nebula is

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.680
the discarded gas, not a planet-forming disk.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.540
Ultraviolet radiation from the hot central star ionizes

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.600
the surrounding gas. That gas produces bright emission

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.140
lines. But a telescope pointed at the central

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:29.820
star also collects its stellar spectrum. So the

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.520
spectrum depends on which region you observe. Move

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.300
the observing window from the gas to the

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.580
star and watch the contribution change.

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.190
Planetary nebulae spread out and fade over tens

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:43.270
of thousands of years. Rings, lobes, and viewing

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:45.550
angle can make their shapes look very different.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:48.310
Companion stars can help shape the outflow; a

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:50.710
particular shape alone does not prove a companion.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:53.750
For this course, follow the stated assumption that

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:55.910
the Sun forms a planetary nebula.
